narrative:
## Tracking Stock with a Map

An inventory maps item names to quantities. When stock arrives, you add. When it sells, you subtract. When checking availability, you look up.

### Basic Inventory Operations

```xiom
fn main() -> Int {
  let inventory: Map[Str, Int] = Map[Str, Int].new();

  inventory.insert("apples", 50);
  inventory.insert("bananas", 30);
  inventory.insert("milk", 15);

  let apple_count = match inventory.get(&"apples") {
    Some(c) => c,
    None => 0,
  };
  io.println("Apples in stock: " + string.int_to_str(apple_count));

  return 0;
}
```

### Adding Stock

When a shipment arrives, add to the existing quantity:

```xiom
fn add_stock(inv: &mut Map[Str, Int], item: Str, amount: Int) {
  let current = match inv.get(&item) {
    Some(c) => c,
    None => 0,
  };
  inv.insert(item, current + amount);
}
```

### Removing Stock

Before selling, check if enough stock exists:

```xiom
fn remove_stock(inv: &mut Map[Str, Int], item: Str, amount: Int) -> Bool {
  let current = match inv.get(&item) {
    Some(c) => c,
    None => 0,
  };
  if current >= amount {
    inv.insert(item, current - amount);
    return true;
  }
  return false;
}

fn main() -> Int {
  var inv: Map[Str, Int] = Map[Str, Int].new();
  inv.insert("apples", 50);
  inv.insert("bananas", 30);

  add_stock(&mut inv, "apples", 20);
  let sold = remove_stock(&mut inv, "bananas", 10);
  io.println("Sale succeeded: " + string.bool_to_str(sold));

  let apples = match inv.get(&"apples") {
    Some(c) => c, None => 0,
  };
  io.println("Apples remaining: " + string.int_to_str(apples));
  return 0;
}
```

This pattern — get current, compute new, insert — is the foundation of inventory systems.

